<section class="block">
<h2>What happened</h2>
<p>Wiz published "Black October" on 30 October, describing a series of security incidents at AI developer and hosting platforms during the month<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. Several platforms attributed their incidents to credential access, and the common thread was stored environment variables holding AI keys; Wiz found no single shared vulnerability and linked key-checking activity to resellers of model access, without claiming they carried out the intrusions<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>. In the same week, a tampered coding extension tried to plant backdoors on developer machines, the kind of foothold that exposes whatever keys a laptop holds<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>.</p>
<p>The pattern has a name. Sysdig described LLMjacking in 2024: attackers use stolen cloud credentials to reach hosted model services, check which keys work, and resell the access, leaving victims with the bill<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>. What has changed is scale: more teams, more platforms and more keys, often with no spending limits.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Why AI keys are different</h2>
<p>A leaked database password is serious, but its damage is bounded by what is in the database. A leaked model key with no limits turns directly into spend, at machine speed, and the first sign is often the invoice. AI keys also spread more widely than most secrets: into notebooks, hosted dev platforms, CI variables, editor settings and personal accounts, because getting started with AI is designed to be easy.</p>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-stack">
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Where keys leak</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Hosted dev platforms<em>environment variables</em></span><span>Laptops and editors<em>extensions, config files</em></span><span>CI and repos<em>variables, history</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Contain</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">AI gateway<em>one route to every model</em></span><span>Per-team keys<em>small blast radius</em></span><span>Short-lived credentials<em>expire quickly</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Cap</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Spend limits<em>per key, per day</em></span><span>Rate limits<em>per key, per model</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Detect</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Usage telemetry<em>tokens by key and model</em></span><span>Anomaly alerts<em>new regions, models, hours</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Respond</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Revoke in minutes<em>from one place</em></span><span>Rotate everywhere<em>scripted</em></span></div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>You cannot stop every leak. You can make each leaked key small, visible and quick to kill.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>A practical playbook</h2>
<p><strong>1. Find every key.</strong> Inventory provider accounts, cloud model services and keys across teams, platforms and CI. Rotate anything stored on a platform named in the Wiz report or any third-party platform you cannot vouch for<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>2. Put a gateway in front of models.</strong> Applications and developers call your gateway, not providers directly. The gateway holds the real provider credentials, issues per-team or per-application keys, and enforces quotas. A key stolen from a laptop is then a key to your gateway, with your limits and your logs. See <a href="/insights/llm-model-routing/">model routing</a> and <a href="/case-studies/uae-software-house-ai-developer-toolchain-security/">securing AI coding tools</a>.</p>
<p><strong>3. Set spend and rate limits per key.</strong> Every key gets a daily budget and an alert well below it. Production keys get headroom; experiment keys do not.</p>
<p><strong>4. Use cloud identity instead of static keys where you can.</strong> For cloud-hosted models, prefer workload identity and short-lived credentials over long-lived access keys, and restrict which models and regions each identity can use. Sysdig's research noted that not every model API call appeared in default cloud audit logs, so check that your logging covers the calls you rely on<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>5. Watch usage like a security signal.</strong> Record tokens, model and caller on every request. The OpenTelemetry generative AI conventions capture model and token usage per call, which gives you the baseline that anomaly alerts need<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-6">6</a></sup>. See <a href="/insights/focus-finops-ai-cost-allocation/">AI cost allocation</a>.</p>
<figure class="fig"><div class="im"><img src="/assets/img/llmjacking-stolen-ai-keys-black-october/token-usage-trace.webp" alt="Jaeger trace of a chat call to a language model showing span tags including requested model, response model, input tokens and output tokens" width="1400" height="622" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>Model and token usage recorded on every call: the baseline that makes stolen-key abuse visible quickly. <span class="credit">Source: <a href="https://opentelemetry.io/blog/2024/otel-generative-ai/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenTelemetry blog, Drew Robbins and Liudmila Molkova</a> · CC BY 4.0</span></figcaption></figure>
<p><strong>6. Rehearse revocation.</strong> Know how to revoke a key, rotate provider credentials behind the gateway and confirm usage stopped, in minutes, outside office hours.</p>
<div class="callout"><p>Treat AI keys like payment cards: issue them per holder, set a limit, watch the statement daily and cancel at the first odd charge.</p></div>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>What to alert on</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<ol class="dgm-flow g3">
<li><b>Baseline</b><span>Normal tokens per key by hour, model and region</span></li>
<li><b>New model</b><span>A key starts calling a model it never used</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Volume</b><span>Usage jumps well above the key's normal range</span></li>
<li><b>Odd hours</b><span>Sustained traffic when the owning team is offline</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>New origin</b><span>Calls from networks or regions the key never used</span></li>
<li><b>Act</b><span>Auto-throttle, page the owner, revoke if unexplained</span></li>
</ol>
<figcaption>Simple rules catch most abuse; the hard part is having per-key data at all.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Who should act</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Any team using hosted AI dev platforms</strong>: rotate keys stored there this week.</li>
<li><strong>Companies with cloud-hosted models</strong>: review which identities can invoke which models, and in which regions.</li>
<li><strong>Finance and FinOps</strong>: add AI spend to daily anomaly checks, not monthly reviews.</li>
<li><strong>Regulated firms</strong>: a stolen key that can reach customer data through a model is a data incident, not just a cost problem; see <a href="/regions/">where I work</a> for regional rules.</li>
</ul>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>CTO take</h2>
<p>October showed that attackers now go after AI keys directly, because those keys turn straight into resellable compute. The fix is not exotic: one gateway, keys per team, limits per key, telemetry per call and practised revocation. Teams that already run AI through a gateway for cost and quality reasons get most of this for free. Teams that do not should start there. For the wider week, see the <a href="/insights/tech-news-2026-11-01/">1 November tech news</a>.</p>
</section>

